\subsection{Cross-VLA Analysis: Model Capability Dependency}
\label{sec:cross_vla}

\looseness=-1 We evaluate ACR-VLA on $\pi_{0.5}$~\cite{black2025pi05} (3.6B, flow matching), which operates at $94$--$99$\% baseline across LIBERO suites. At this near-expert performance level, both CRM and Adaptive correction produce negative results. A $\tau$-sweep on Goal reveals the mechanism: at $\tau{=}0.90$ (the most conservative threshold tested), the gate triggers on only $0.35$\% of steps---effectively disabling CRM entirely---yet success rate still drops from $96$\% to $89$\%. Since CRM fires on fewer than 1 in 300 steps, the $-$7\,pp degradation cannot be attributed to correction quality.

\looseness=-1 The cause is argmin-to-centroid selection itself. $K{=}2$ mean (averaging without argmin selection) achieves $96.5$\%, matching the VLA-only baseline. Argmin selection, which chooses the sample closest to the centroid, introduces a selection bias: it systematically favors lower-variance actions, distorting a distribution that is already well-calibrated. For SmolVLA (0.4B), where the action distribution is noisier, this filtering is beneficial---argmin removes outlier samples. For $\pi_{0.5}$ (3.6B), where the base policy is near-expert, argmin disrupts the natural action distribution without improving it.

\looseness=-1 This reveals a model capability dependency: ACR-VLA's benefit is concentrated in the regime where the base VLA has room for improvement. Near-expert VLAs do not benefit from post-hoc correction, and the selection step itself can be harmful. A boundary analysis on OpenVLA-OFT~\cite{kim2024openvla} (7B, deterministic $L_1$ regression) identifies a second boundary: deterministic VLAs produce near-identical samples ($97.3$\% of steps $L_2 < 0.01$), making multi-sample methods vacuous (Appendix~\ref{sec:applicability}).

